```python
def branch_offset(word):
    def bits(hi, lo):
        return (word >> lo) & ((1 << (hi - lo + 1)) - 1)
    imm = bits(31, 31) << 12 | bits(7, 7) << 11 | bits(30, 25) << 5 | bits(11, 8) << 1
    return imm - (1 << 13) if imm >> 12 else imm
```

In hardware this is pure wiring plus sign extension, exactly the `imm_b` line of the lesson's Verilog: `{{19{instr[31]}}, instr[31], instr[7], instr[30:25], instr[11:8], 1'b0}`. The range is $-4096$ to $+4094$ bytes, so a conditional branch reaches about a thousand instructions either way; farther targets need a branch over a `jal`. For `0x7a7346e3` the offset is $+4012$, which needs imm[11], stored in instruction bit 7, to be set.

